### 8a. Sprint 19.26 (2026-09-09 → 2026-09-22) — bug root-cause analysis: why did these closed bugs slip through?

*What this shows: for each bug the team fixed this sprint — flagging which came in as customer/partner **Support** tickets and who reported them — a trace from the fix back to the pull request(s) that last touched that same code, who reviewed that last touch, the people who wrote it, a best-effort guess at whether the buggy code was AI- or human-authored, a **leadership-level** explanation of the process gap that let it slip through, the prevention steps the fix already applied, and the follow-on actions still recommended. Use it to spot recurring process gaps — missing acceptance criteria, skipped TRDs, thin test coverage, over-trusting AI, single-reviewer merges — and target prevention. (Scope: closed bugs that shipped a code change; bugs closed without code aren't analyzable here.)*

None of the five bugs below carried the `Support` tag — all were internally found.

#### auro-formkit

- **[1510603](https://dev.azure.com/itsals/E_Retain_Content/_workitems/edit/1510603) — Fix missing footer buttons in auro-counter fullscreen view on the API demo page**
  - **Fix:** [PR #1618](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-formkit/pull/1618) — adds the missing `import '@aurodesignsystem/auro-button'` to the counter demo's `customize.js` so the fullscreen bib's footer buttons actually upgrade and render, alongside a broader counter-group validity refactor (`computeValidityFromCounters()` extracted from `updateValidity()`, `renderHelpTextErrors()` removed, `auroFormElement-validated` documented).
  - **Last touched by:** [PR #1595](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-formkit/pull/1595) — 2026-08-31. **Contributors:** Stephen Rios (65 lines), Jason Baker (65), rmenner (17), Eunsun Mota (12), Jordan Jones (1).
  - **Who reviewed the last touch (#1595):** Eunsun Mota (requested changes) and Jason Baker (commented) — two humans engaged, which is the healthiest review on this list. Sourcery, Chromatic and GitHub Actions also commented; those are bots and do not count as human review.
  - **Last touched by AI/Human — guess (low/med/high):** guess — AI-assisted (med): the closest prior commit `ad5dca8e` ("fix: correct CEM types and add deprecation flags for editor IntelliSense AB#1633053") carries a `Co-authored-by AI: Claude Opus 4.8` trailer; the surrounding commits from Stephen Rios and Jason Baker have `trailers=NONE` and read as hand-written.
  - **Why it slipped through:** the demo page is treated as documentation rather than product, so nothing in the team's definition of done requires a demo page to be opened and exercised before a component change ships. A missing custom-element registration produces no build error and no test failure — it only shows as an invisible button to a human looking at the page. The prior PR was a metadata/IntelliSense pass with no acceptance criterion covering "the demo still renders," so two competent human reviewers had no reason to catch it.
  - **Regression Remedies Applied:** substantial. The fix added counter-group unit coverage (`components/counter/test/auro-counter-group.test.js`, +232), a Storybook regression story (`components/counter/stories/component.stories.ts`, +45), a shared Playwright-facing suite update (`apps/shared/form-interaction.suite.ts`, +34), a manual-testing doc update, and a post-mortem (`docs/post-mortem/1642340.md`, +69). Note the post-mortem is filed under a *different* work item number than this bug.
  - **Follow-on Recommendations:** add a lightweight smoke check that loads each component's `demo/` and `apiExamples/` pages and fails when a referenced `auro-*` tag never upgrades — this class of defect is invisible to unit tests by construction. Extend the `code-review` skill to flag PRs that add an `auro-*` tag to a demo/example file without a matching import. Reconcile the post-mortem filename with the bug ID so the trail is followable.

- **[1616087](https://dev.azure.com/itsals/E_Retain_Content/_workitems/edit/1616087) — Stop shipping handlebars and markdown-magic as runtime dependencies in auro-library**
  - **⚠ Data-quality caveat — mislinked fix:** the ticket links two PRs, but `auro-library#1615` does not exist (404) — that number belongs to the auro-formkit range. Only [PR #296](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-library/pull/296) is real and is the actual fix; the analysis below rests on #296.
  - **Fix:** [PR #296](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-library/pull/296) — pre-compiles the doc-generation scripts into a bundled build (`build/bundleDocsScripts.mjs` +131, `build/entryPoints.mjs` +141, a `syncRequest` CommonJS shim +113) so `handlebars` and `markdown-magic` move out of runtime `dependencies`.
  - **Last touched by:** [PR #73](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-library/pull/73) — 2024-10-21. **Contributors:** Chris Friedberg (52 lines), Dale Sande (17), Jason Baker (2).
  - **Who reviewed the last touch (#73):** Jason Baker (approved) and Dale Sande (commented) — one human approver. Sourcery also commented; it is a bot and does not count as human review. *Review gap: single human approver on a change to shared build scripts consumed by every Auro component.*
  - **Last touched by AI/Human — guess (low/med/high):** guess — human-written (med): all four prior commits show ordinary human authors with `trailers=NONE`, and the October 2024 date predates the team's AI-assisted commit conventions.
  - **Why it slipped through:** this is a packaging defect, not a logic defect, and the team has no gate that asks "what does a consumer actually download?" The build scripts and the runtime bundle share one `package.json`, so a dependency added for doc generation silently became a runtime dependency for every consuming component. No acceptance criterion in the original work mentioned dependency surface or install size, and a single approver reviewing a build-script change had no signal that the published artifact had changed.
  - **Regression Remedies Applied:** the strongest remediation of the five. The fix added four dedicated test suites — `build/__tests__/distBundle.spec.js` (+405), `build/__tests__/packaging.spec.js` (+123), `build/__tests__/syncRequestShim.spec.js` (+116), `scripts/utils/__tests__/ansiColors.spec.js` (+220) — plus a test fixture server and a post-mortem (`docs/post-mortem/1616087.md`, +164). The new `packaging.spec.js` directly guards the regression.
  - **Follow-on Recommendations:** promote the new packaging assertion into a shared CI check that every Auro repo runs, not just auro-library — the same dev-vs-runtime dependency mistake is possible in all of them. Require two human approvers on changes to `package.json` dependency blocks and shared build scripts, given the blast radius. Fix the stale `#1615` link on the ADO work item.

#### auro-toast

- **[1376974](https://dev.azure.com/itsals/E_Retain_Content/_workitems/edit/1376974) — Toast close event is named with an "on" prefix, breaking handler binding in Svelte**
  - **Fix:** [PR #147](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-toast/pull/147) — adds a standards-compliant `toast-close` CustomEvent with a `{ visible, id }` detail alongside the legacy `onToastClose` (now marked `@deprecated` but still dispatched for backwards compatibility), plus a standalone live region and other a11y improvements.
  - **Last touched by:** [PR #116](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-toast/pull/116) — 2026-05-07. **Contributors:** Dale Sande (218 lines), Eunsun Mota (71), Jason Baker (31), cynthiaricomendoza-alaskaair (15), Doug (14), Jordan Jones (8), Forrest Taylor (2).
  - **Who reviewed the last touch (#116):** **no human reviewed it** — the only recorded participant is GitHub Actions, a bot. *Review gap: zero human approvers.* In fairness, #116 is a release-candidate roll-up ("RC #115") rather than a feature PR, but that means the naming decision was never reviewed at any point in the chain that blame reaches.
  - **Last touched by AI/Human — guess (low/med/high):** guess — human-written (med-high): the blame-adjacent commits are all ordinary human authors with `trailers=NONE`. The one AI trailer present (`Co-authored-by AI: Claude Sonnet 4.6 (1M context)` on `c1d35369`) is on *this sprint's fix*, not on the code that introduced the defect.
  - **Why it slipped through:** this is an API-design gap, not a coding error. `onToastClose` was a valid-looking name that only breaks when a consumer uses a framework whose event binding assumes DOM conventions — Svelte's `on:` prefix collides with it. Nothing in the team's process tests Auro components against the frameworks that actually consume them, and there is no written event-naming standard for a reviewer to check a new event against. It took a downstream consumer hitting it in Svelte to surface a naming choice made years earlier.
  - **Regression Remedies Applied:** good coverage. The fix added substantial unit tests (`test/auro-toast.test.js`, +163), a post-mortem (`docs/post-mortem/1376974.md`, +81), and updated the API docs and README to document the deprecation path. The legacy event is retained, so consumers are not broken. No framework-integration (Svelte/React) test was added — the exact scenario that produced the bug remains untested.
  - **Follow-on Recommendations:** write an event-naming standard into the `coding-standards` skill (no `on*` prefixes on dispatched CustomEvents; kebab-case, component-scoped) and have the `code-review` skill flag any new `dispatchEvent` whose name starts with `on`. Extend the existing `auro-formkit` framework-integration Playwright apps to cover standalone components like toast, so Svelte/React binding is exercised in CI. Audit the other Auro components for remaining `on*`-named events and deprecate them on the same schedule.

#### auro-hyperlink

- **[1551053](https://dev.azure.com/itsals/E_Retain_Content/_workitems/edit/1551053) — Fix hyperlink internal/same-domain example rendering the external-link icon**
  - **⚠ Data-quality caveat — partially mislinked:** the ticket links two PRs; `auro-hyperlink#364` does not exist (404). [PR #344](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-hyperlink/pull/344) is real and is the fix analyzed here.
  - **Fix:** [PR #344](https://github.com/AlaskaAirlines/auro-hyperlink/pull/344) — "docs: fix same domain example to point same host": corrects the `href` in `apiExamples/external.html` and `external-appearance-inverse.html` so the "same domain" example actually points at the same host, and bumps `src/iconVersion.js` from `9.1.1` to `9.2.0`. Documentation/example content only — no component logic changed.
  - **Last touched by:** no prior PR resolved — the buggy lines were net-new in the fix's own history or could not be attributed. **Contributors:** Eunsun Mota (1 line).
  - **Who reviewed the last touch:** no prior PR to attribute review to.
  - **Last touched by AI/Human — guess (low/med/high):** guess — human-written (low): the single blame commit `c1692fea` (authored by Eunsun Mota, committed by Ryan Menner) shows `trailers=NONE`, and a one-line `href` in a hand-maintained HTML example is characteristic of manual editing. Only one line of prior code exists to judge, hence low confidence.
  - **Why it slipped through:** example HTML is not covered by any automated check, and nobody is accountable for verifying that an example demonstrates what its heading claims. The "same domain" example pointed at a different host, so the component behaved *correctly* by rendering the external-link icon — the documentation was wrong, not the code. Reviewers reading a docs diff have no way to notice a semantically wrong URL without opening the page.
  - **Regression Remedies Applied:** **none — the fix added no new safety net.** The changed files are two example HTML pages, `src/iconVersion.js`, and dependency manifests. No test, no Storybook story, no post-mortem.
  - **Follow-on Recommendations:** add an assertion to the docs build (or the demo smoke check recommended for 1510603) that each hyperlink example renders the icon state its heading advertises. Ask the `ado` skill to generate an explicit "the example demonstrates the stated behavior" acceptance criterion for docs/example tickets. Correct the stale `#364` link on the work item.

#### auro-popover

- **[1311537](https://dev.azure.com/itsals/E_Retain_Content/_workitems/edit/1311537) — Expose auro-popover arrow and inner elements as styleable shadow parts**
  - **⚠ Data-quality caveat — mislinked fix:** the only PR on this ticket, `auro-popover#135`, does not exist on GitHub (404). With no resolvable fix PR there is no diff to read, nothing to blame, and no reviewers to attribute — the analysis below is therefore limited to what the ticket itself says.
  - **Not analyzed:** no resolvable fix PR (ADO link points at a PR number that does not exist in `AlaskaAirlines/auro-popover`). The real fix is likely linked elsewhere in the repo or was merged without an `AB#1311537` mention in the PR body.
  - **Follow-on Recommendations:** re-link the correct pull request on the ADO work item so this bug can be traced. More broadly, three of five bugs this sprint carry an unresolvable PR link — see the summary below.

#### How the team can ship more reliably

- **Demo and example surfaces have no automated gate (2 of 5 bugs — 1510603, 1551053).** Both defects lived in `demo/`/`apiExamples/` content that compiles cleanly, passes every unit test, and is only wrong to a human looking at the rendered page. **Action:** add a demo/example smoke check to CI that loads each page, fails when a referenced `auro-*` tag never upgrades, and asserts examples demonstrate their stated behavior.
- **No framework-integration testing for standalone components (1 of 5 — 1376974).** The toast event-naming bug is definitionally invisible to WTR tests and only appears when a Svelte consumer binds a handler. **Action:** extend the framework-integration Playwright apps that already exist for `auro-formkit` to cover standalone components.
- **Missing written standards for API surface (1 of 5 — 1376974).** There is no event-naming rule for a reviewer to check against, so `onToastClose` passed review as a reasonable-looking name. **Action:** encode event-naming and public-API conventions in the `coding-standards` skill and have `code-review` enforce them mechanically.
- **Thin review on high-blast-radius changes (2 of 5 — 1616087 zero-to-one human approver on shared build scripts, 1376974 zero human reviewers on the last touch).** **Action:** require two human approvers on changes to shared build scripts and `package.json` dependency blocks; treat AI reviewers (Sourcery, Copilot) as advisory only, never as the approval of record.
- **Work-item link hygiene is degrading (3 of 5 bugs — 1311537, 1551053, 1616087 all carry PR links that 404).** This is not a code problem but it directly blocks root-cause analysis: one bug this sprint could not be analyzed at all. **Action:** make an `AB#<id>` mention in the PR body part of the PR template, and add a periodic job that flags ADO work items whose linked PR numbers don't resolve.
- **AI authorship is not itself the pattern.** Only one of the five bugs traces to AI-assisted code, and in the toast case the AI trailer is on the *fix*, not the defect. The recurring theme is absent automated gates and thin review, not over-trusting AI.

